}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-11}{-4}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{5}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{3},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(11,-3,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-4,5,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=11-4t \\\\ y=-3+5t \\\\ z=-1+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-70866/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9927bff1-c245-542b-8d5d-fa7d24188b85"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-70866"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71023/selection/75fdec6e15aed6ec7d4df941c816121c699171cb80b9665aff4ecd336b1247a3" :answer/id #uuid "583567b6-abea-5553-8324-858f3f94504a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-2}{3}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71023/selection/fdb2b5422c89b5aa813c57ffa28b12d9b346da4aab8703337c98483829c3c480" :answer/id #uuid "740c76de-9afb-570c-ba3b-b18c5bb775fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{3}=\\frac{z}{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71023/selection/619ecfb87035fd3745b96e2af20a918f629e924331f1cd73fbaffbdcafd80774" :answer/id #uuid "c59402fb-1162-57b3-94a0-e01189176ba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{1}=\\frac{y-3}{-2}=\\frac{z+1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71023/selection/77426a6cc47c23d66e4a2878d6420b1e6b89577976a40f08a4938a9364f4237e" :answer/id #uuid "9ba6d22f-b743-504d-b7de-3ecbd38867ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{1}=\\frac{y+3}{-2}=\\frac{z-1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71023/selection/9f225f10ff5472ec0ffa6282f7a89da6e109707b58919380c87d27d0630e5af3" :answer/id #uuid "059fbaa9-51ba-5bb8-a252-49a1ae4644bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{4}=\\frac{y+2}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71023/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "79529f7a-c7cd-5032-ac14-f1a9b411957b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71023/selection/9f225f10ff5472ec0ffa6282f7a89da6e109707b58919380c87d27d0630e5af3" :answer-field/choices ["q-71023/selection/75fdec6e15aed6ec7d4df941c816121c699171cb80b9665aff4ecd336b1247a3" "q-71023/selection/fdb2b5422c89b5aa813c57ffa28b12d9b346da4aab8703337c98483829c3c480" "q-71023/selection/619ecfb87035fd3745b96e2af20a918f629e924331f1cd73fbaffbdcafd80774" "q-71023/selection/77426a6cc47c23d66e4a2878d6420b1e6b89577976a40f08a4938a9364f4237e" "q-71023/selection/9f225f10ff5472ec0ffa6282f7a89da6e109707b58919380c87d27d0630e5af3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71023" :question/id #uuid "bdb4f9b9-5e7d-5a5f-aa2b-84ba59f680dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-71023" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(1,-2,3)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨4,3,-1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{4}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{4}=\\frac{y+2}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71023/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71023"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71028/selection/a55a1ddb4736bb09e7c1f9d72256ddc9dedca44ef6c6b0431391e2263d22c64d" :answer/id #uuid "229ba633-5666-56b5-aa04-c5d5e2a1c4f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+10}{3}=\\frac{y}{-7}=\\frac{z-6}{-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71028/selection/0ac6d3042ac913b935e9bee983414564c7f44cb5a93a90de80600404ab47bd32" :answer/id #uuid "f9a60d36-3063-56da-95e0-7a6d3d372bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-10}{3}=\\frac{y}{-7}=\\frac{z+6}{-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71028/selection/957fde5235a3187bdb6a01f8207d3110b350f13e86238cc2e8c34e5ba625f0e0" :answer/id #uuid "aa0d19aa-34fd-5898-9edc-37f4f71e3b7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{3}=\\frac{y}{-7}=\\frac{z}{-5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71028/selection/6348700055a6527d0b7cdb21378972cd4278c8a575149d697de4168b7080826e" :answer/id #uuid "d5e9af64-9a5d-5967-9b7e-9bb48ec3a344" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{10}=\\frac{z-5}{-6},\\,y=7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71028/selection/5cf569100f5d0fec0701d782e2563b781e29ebe9d9a4970cd452711d61bd4de8" :answer/id #uuid "343987ad-0cb8-58c0-a62c-fd698944a163" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{10}=\\frac{z+5}{-6},\\,y=-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71028/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9b0d63-8f0a-512d-b324-d777cb00bd76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71028/selection/0ac6d3042ac913b935e9bee983414564c7f44cb5a93a90de80600404ab47bd32" :answer-field/choices ["q-71028/selection/a55a1ddb4736bb09e7c1f9d72256ddc9dedca44ef6c6b0431391e2263d22c64d" "q-71028/selection/0ac6d3042ac913b935e9bee983414564c7f44cb5a93a90de80600404ab47bd32" "q-71028/selection/957fde5235a3187bdb6a01f8207d3110b350f13e86238cc2e8c34e5ba625f0e0" "q-71028/selection/6348700055a6527d0b7cdb21378972cd4278c8a575149d697de4168b7080826e" "q-71028/selection/5cf569100f5d0fec0701d782e2563b781e29ebe9d9a4970cd452711d61bd4de8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71028" :question/id #uuid "44e58ff9-1a7d-5505-85a5-4d9ca86c2073" :question/math-academy-id "q-71028" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(10,0,-6)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨3,-7,-5⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{3}=\\frac{y-0}{-7}=\\frac{z-(-6)}{-5}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{3}=\\frac{y}{-7}=\\frac{z+6}{-5}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71028/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71028"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127405/selection/1960289a6b00cc237032028890d2011eb8739bad00273fc1d5cae42adfdd3d60" :answer/id #uuid "caf2213b-8990-5f57-be89-4a114b928416" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+6}{-2}=\\frac{y-3}{1}=\\frac{z+2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127405/selection/07e6b84f22a30751f3a19530f2f09f4ac99c76eab6a523d85c8bd845fb5776f5" :answer/id #uuid "7f2a53d4-da7f-5c51-bcb3-b5010b14fcfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+6}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{1}=\\frac{z-2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127405/selection/475bb97335c3934ffafb9b520377be52eedb5f983c532a69007739804753c1ad" :answer/id #uuid "5b38264f-ecf1-5a42-8f45-af5115520f9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z+5}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127405/selection/1564bd14a188759e7bb752ddb3120eebe300a0f183fb01585f7da33a52d35688" :answer/id #uuid "bc978f72-8a6b-5b3c-81cc-2332f1fc5336" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127405/selection/6fda03bf34075a7fbadb3edd0513ac02490a48704e9d629ece672049b5f93bc2" :answer/id #uuid "ed911f19-4550-5391-823e-ea0256a45a7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z+5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127405/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ed095a53-9e46-565f-8300-a51fe81037fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127405/selection/6fda03bf34075a7fbadb3edd0513ac02490a48704e9d629ece672049b5f93bc2" :answer-field/choices ["q-127405/selection/1960289a6b00cc237032028890d2011eb8739bad00273fc1d5cae42adfdd3d60" "q-127405/selection/07e6b84f22a30751f3a19530f2f09f4ac99c76eab6a523d85c8bd845fb5776f5" "q-127405/selection/475bb97335c3934ffafb9b520377be52eedb5f983c532a69007739804753c1ad" "q-127405/selection/1564bd14a188759e7bb752ddb3120eebe300a0f183fb01585f7da33a52d35688" "q-127405/selection/6fda03bf34075a7fbadb3edd0513ac02490a48704e9d629ece672049b5f93bc2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127405" :question/id #uuid "60027f0f-2705-55d1-a629-b924e3a07c06" :question/math-academy-id "q-127405" :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2-6t \\\\ y=1+3t \\\\ z=-5+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-6t \\\\ 1+3t \\\\ -5+2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,1,-5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-6,3,2⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-(-5)}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z+5}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-127405/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d44cf726-a4a6-5ac9-a63c-7464fe5566c5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127405"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127461/selection/a5794425c524bea0bf85d7ad1904e2374524e3e367d7595715b06de8f87befd5" :answer/id #uuid "34b48131-54c7-5b7e-8fd7-6719c523d2cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127461/selection/d74a54a62cba8c1b345edf21b8eb2249130bef54875578db9b2c9e696ec1b0f8" :answer/id #uuid "e62976fc-be6a-521a-8900-90e666d78d62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y}{2},\\,z=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127461/selection/ebc399e129c33bf267743ed0899e77b27ebc3ce624a0fd338a28afd38729663b" :answer/id #uuid "39648397-ac79-5257-a04b-ee3ac53a75cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y}{-1},\\,z=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127461/selection/8220381beedfbacfd783f31cd005ed107d6ed88cb5b6c5b7dd04589dd9e88577" :answer/id #uuid "1ae647c9-2750-50b8-94dc-37a5dfdd88c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-5}{-3}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127461/selection/5479028ebe4dbf1bf1b70d6c1d435d2341a0458efe2fd2de82a78951926283fc" :answer/id #uuid "241f7184-cd1c-5da9-8386-f0e2288172c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127461/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "03ace7f6-72b8-5235-9985-6e4949cbed7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127461/selection/a5794425c524bea0bf85d7ad1904e2374524e3e367d7595715b06de8f87befd5" :answer-field/choices ["q-127461/selection/a5794425c524bea0bf85d7ad1904e2374524e3e367d7595715b06de8f87befd5" "q-127461/selection/d74a54a62cba8c1b345edf21b8eb2249130bef54875578db9b2c9e696ec1b0f8" "q-127461/selection/ebc399e129c33bf267743ed0899e77b27ebc3ce624a0fd338a28afd38729663b" "q-127461/selection/8220381beedfbacfd783f31cd005ed107d6ed88cb5b6c5b7dd04589dd9e88577" "q-127461/selection/5479028ebe4dbf1bf1b70d6c1d435d2341a0458efe2fd2de82a78951926283fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127461" :question/id #uuid "ee1c5089-3856-5f69-9d8f-cf952b30e484" :question/math-academy-id "q-127461" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1}=\\frac{z+2}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z+2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=-2:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3-5t \\\\ t \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z+2}{0}$ part and instead write $z=-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=-2\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-127461/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07c209a4-5c69-561e-9015-28b0f6e436e5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127461"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-32829/selection/fece30fd1186a84588229d76d56b597c912266782475e65ffb9c9a7f0f9341e8" :answer/id #uuid "a6a2164a-df74-5cf2-98b2-37cd74d35d64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{-1}=\\frac{y+3}{1}=\\frac{z+2}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-32829/selection/35fb9ad58b3c68865328827b639d08223ce77986f338a08ccca2226d0e9714cf" :answer/id #uuid "aa987b38-fe87-598c-8a90-cf69df562a89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{4}=\\frac{y+1}{-3}=\\frac{z+4}{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-32829/selection/5813a08aefba8fc939a64c1be9768faa9066149bf2b23204b67b9dbce53e98a7" :answer/id #uuid "b674db99-426c-5d95-824e-2cba647096db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+4}{-1}=\\frac{y-3}{1}=\\frac{z-2}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-32829/selection/4d30ccd03934acebee0fd56949b7bc342986da9ff45ca5b33be4048edc228a76" :answer/id #uuid "834ec128-bd5c-5391-8d02-ae7b075c17ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{-3}=\\frac{z}{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-32829/selection/fb848c77335d43b1bc414f598de297b68d7afb04fa28c2a44523684cf8985f25" :answer/id #uuid "df691d90-b64e-59a3-8cb5-791279299360" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-32829/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3847d5b1-c8a9-5f3c-91ef-af3a2046d41c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-32829/selection/fb848c77335d43b1bc414f598de297b68d7afb04fa28c2a44523684cf8985f25" :answer-field/choices ["q-32829/selection/fece30fd1186a84588229d76d56b597c912266782475e65ffb9c9a7f0f9341e8" "q-32829/selection/35fb9ad58b3c68865328827b639d08223ce77986f338a08ccca2226d0e9714cf" "q-32829/selection/5813a08aefba8fc939a64c1be9768faa9066149bf2b23204b67b9dbce53e98a7" "q-32829/selection/4d30ccd03934acebee0fd56949b7bc342986da9ff45ca5b33be4048edc228a76" "q-32829/selection/fb848c77335d43b1bc414f598de297b68d7afb04fa28c2a44523684cf8985f25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-32829" :question/id #uuid "34db07db-3de7-53c3-98b1-614df6d6ac22" :question/math-academy-id "q-32829" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/58076d96d149ff4521103fd15c7187bc1a58595ab20f28be92be0f79385aee61.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,1,4)$ and $Q(3,-2,2).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-32829/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cbc74baa-313b-51e7-8ae1-5e9fe0761256"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-32829"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-33234"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/1f9599192c1979f40fc519458019ed3296d9a5ef9048aaed41aab973c62e0be8.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-127527"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/8722b93655e069e2f35147b572c16ed7af408a86f8a88597c79aea8fe59e9c4d.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-127466"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/1647eb5cca32c6fdc99e23507db151a8299b29bfa70ebfbaa941e17fe4638586.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5021"] :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/4b3aa22ba1342c45d422c227258837ae080ee705f2580437c775bd2beaf8afeb.png)" :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/cafe7ac2b6224a50a288cabf6b6838c6a85a722404c9ebd20238f689f196b841.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4912"] :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/792bf09ff2546903b863a879a2518c42bbd30fdfce652bd4e22b1ad19aefc7c9.png)" :question/worked-solution "We know that the line passes through the points ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/8f40930bbc65b25e23296d4a6fae2bb12da4713b1b65a0444ed6aedb8287f857.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/10c3f2aff07e3e42290e5fddc4751c493a0ca3839907c32a627bcbdbcc6c8aa8.png) Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/6c751380dede71816d826d31daf38dbcd4f34b6b462de00ed5071bd366a6645c.png) which is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/e3ae667c43eafd8804a9fb7033c9040c5d4cca11b0c90368f7dc5a7cfda35934.png)\n\nUsing the point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/c9b75fa5f325825788ae2a64e227779c81af2454230ded498d7245dd9e0c66cf.png) and the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/dee4f9a47a1bdff7b5bee4b328768e8967ff52dba70800b6defa0575a7034847.png), we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/8f28616529da6a43199b0f9bfe74c186544f84c111f58ae43d066c2089acc921.png)\nor equivalently\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/158ba3699f056c1d67ae3c56699c266a4cc99c6e0d5d13f70dae21c04a5f54bf.png)\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/9d8d4e18a0bed3777184a8299ba2f3183c3a43740f75103f18e2e910f27f8b40.png) involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/d0a37c7a07a4a5e017e3edb659fad8defff4fa33a3accbd0e6fd2d6d8439cf94.png) component always maintains the constant value ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/b7f9100157159abea8f5f7a86a272d5a539e2d8084e8c894642bc49342e2a76f.png) For example, our two points ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/ad5d9528b6a85fa9d0e010a6c7b0fe7d51253b71fe995a2010204708256aa081.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/5a762bf2a30c7fb3be3ba2e1d8abf1b308614d123e11600d93d7b3b02fe828c7.png) both have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/fdc688a7d7010425aace3db801008b0b83b950e7ff7c03a89f9745d4651e1dca.png)-coordinates of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/e80fbe48c17ecbcad07d73a5822a6bc6c86aa7e20cbc7df457daebbcc98d3ccb.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/634bc23451d22fdadb3de9c4d9fc8c4559e1b79cefaf5771b541373a33f3ef9a.png) part and instead write ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/c8e5605672eaf10428132773817bccd6795c20281686d5bcb272370619b4b81d.png) as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/6f956b080575cea02674247877e555d3158f184f55bff45147011be97c4b474b.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
